# continuation-of.rq (loop M, 2026-08-02) — THE PAGINATION LAW.
#
# Open-world derivation (CLAUDE.md §8): "page N's table CONTINUES page N-1's table" is GROWN from
# evidence both pages carry — the header block the renderer repeated when it split one logical
# table across a page break. Every input is a presence/equality fact emitted by
# document.continuation_evidence (float equalities pre-applied there with the established
# geometry.COORD_EPS epsilon, exactly as ruledroles does), so this query carries NO numeric
# literal and no tolerance. A page that evidences nothing stays independent: recognition is
# licensed, never assumed from page adjacency.
#
# READING vs COMPARING (state this plainly). The law compares two renderings of the SAME string
# for literal identity. It draws no meaning from any string — no keyword, no month name, no
# "looks like a header" judgment. Asking "did the renderer draw this again?" is evidence
# comparison; asking "what does this say?" would be reading, and would belong to NEURAL (§8).
#
# THE LAW — page N continues page N-1 iff ALL of:
#   (a) both pages present a leaf header row (at least one leaf cell each, each column at most
#       once), and
#   (b) every column of page N-1's leaf row has a counterpart at the SAME column index on page N
#       carrying the SAME text (literal identity — no normalisation, no fuzzy match) at an
#       AGREEING ink origin, and
#   (c) the mirror: no column of page N's leaf row is left without such a counterpart — so an
#       extra or missing column on either page refuses, and
#   (d) the two pages' AUTHOR-DRAWN leaf-grid boundaries agree AS SETS, under COORD_EPS: every
#       boundary on each side has an agreeing counterpart on the other. Set-wise, not one-for-one
#       — a boundary drawn twice at the same x is tolerated, which is correct (it is one mark).
# Any partial match refuses. There is no "mostly the same header".
#
# WHAT THIS LAW CANNOT SEPARATE — THE CASE-2 / CASE-3 BOUNDARY (honest, §7; residue R33)
# The evidence is the REPEATED HEADER BLOCK, and two logically INDEPENDENT tables built from one
# template share that evidence exactly. Measured (loop M task 2 review, synthetic 2-page PDF):
# same `Store | Item | Qty` header at the same grid, DIFFERENT data, even different per-page
# banners — the law stitches them, and tab:continuesTable is asserted between two tables that are
# not one table. That is taxonomy case 3 (spec §2b defers it), and this law does not decide it:
# case 2 and case 3 are indistinguishable from the header block ALONE.
# A discriminator IS available and is NOT text reading: the BODY side. A paginated table is cut
# BY the page edge, so page N-1's table runs to that page's LAST text line; an independent table
# ends before it, with a banner or whitespace after. That is a presence/ordering fact over lines
# already extracted — no threshold, no distance. The discriminator must FIRST exclude blocks that
# repeat on EVERY page (page-invariant furniture — the same repeated-block evidence the law already
# emits), THEN ask whether the earlier table's last body row ends the page. See R33 for the full
# statement; until it lands, every caller must treat recognition as "the header repeats", not "these
# are one table".
#
# CLAUSE (d)'s COMPARISON BASIS — MEASURED, not assumed (specimen: the 3-page GrainCorp stem,
# corpus/ag-trade/graincorp-stem-2026-07-31.pdf, measured 2026-08-02):
#   * the RAW page rule x-set is NOT comparable: page 0 carries an extra mark at x=832.32 that
#     pages 1-2 do not (the table's right border is drawn twice, at 832.08/832.32/833.04). It
#     bounds no column any ink occupies, and grid recovery already drops it — comparing raw rule
#     sets would refuse a genuine continuation over a duplicated border stroke.
#   * the FULL leaf-grid boundary set is NOT comparable either: loop G's header-confirmed
#     boundaries are inferred from THIS page's ink, and they MOVE — the confirmed gutter before
#     the last column measured 798.54 (p0) / 802.04 (p1) / 792.04 (p2). Comparing them would
#     refuse every pair. (Per-leaf-cell column-left-x fails for the same reason: the last
#     column's left edge IS that inferred gutter.)
#   * the AUTHOR-DRAWN leaf-grid boundaries DO agree, exactly, on both pairs (16 of 18
#     boundaries; the two dropped are precisely loop G's inferred pair — 751.54, which happens to
#     agree on all three pages, and the gutter, which does not). That is the basis this clause
#     uses: a mark the author drew is a property of the TABLE, a boundary the pipeline inferred is
#     a property of ONE PAGE'S INK. Only the former can be compared across a break — and an
#     inferred boundary that happens to agree (751.54) is a coincidence of this document's ink,
#     not evidence about the author's grid, so it is excluded for the same reason as the one that
#     moves.
#   * a second reason to restrict to author marks, which matters independently: R32 (loop G can
#     FABRICATE a boundary inside spanning ink that contains a space). A fabricated boundary
#     carries no rule, so it can never enter this clause — clause (d) is immune to R32 by
#     construction.
# Clause (d) is VACUOUS when neither page presents an author-drawn boundary (a wholly inferred
# grid): open world, no author mark to contradict. That is deliberate — the clause can only speak
# where the author left marks — and clauses (b)/(c) still bind column for column.
# Column POSITION is therefore carried a second way, per column, by tab:originAgreesWith on the
# leaf cells themselves (clause b/c): where the author's own ink starts is an author fact too,
# and it is what discriminates a shifted grid carrying the same labels.
#
# ROLE OF THE PAGE INDEX: none. Adjacency is chosen by the caller (the table that ENDS page N-1
# against the table that OPENS page N); the law reads only the two header blocks it is given.
PREFIX tab: <https://w3id.org/iladub/tab#>
SELECT ?prior ?continuation WHERE {
  ?prior a tab:PriorPageBlock .
  ?continuation a tab:ContinuationPageBlock .
  # CLAUSE (a) — both leaf rows exist. Positive evidence: a page with no recoverable leaf header
  # row evidences nothing, so it never continues and is never continued.
  FILTER EXISTS { ?prior tab:hasLeafCell ?anyPrior }
  FILTER EXISTS { ?continuation tab:hasLeafCell ?anyCur }
  # ...and each column index is claimed at most once per page, or "column for column" is not
  # well-defined and the derivation refuses (the loop-L clause-1 idiom, stated as presence).
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    ?prior tab:hasLeafCell ?pa . ?pa tab:leafColumnIndex ?dupP .
    ?prior tab:hasLeafCell ?pb . ?pb tab:leafColumnIndex ?dupP .
    FILTER(?pa != ?pb)
  }
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    ?continuation tab:hasLeafCell ?ca . ?ca tab:leafColumnIndex ?dupC .
    ?continuation tab:hasLeafCell ?cb . ?cb tab:leafColumnIndex ?dupC .
    FILTER(?ca != ?cb)
  }
  # CLAUSE (b) — every prior column is matched: same index, identical text (the ?t join IS the
  # identity test), agreeing origin.
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    ?prior tab:hasLeafCell ?pc .
    ?pc tab:leafColumnIndex ?i .
    FILTER NOT EXISTS {
      ?continuation tab:hasLeafCell ?cc .
      ?cc tab:leafColumnIndex ?i .
      ?pc tab:leafCellText ?t .
      ?cc tab:leafCellText ?t .
      ?pc tab:originAgreesWith ?cc .
    }
  }
  # CLAUSE (c) — the mirror, so neither page may carry a column the other lacks.
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    ?continuation tab:hasLeafCell ?cc2 .
    ?cc2 tab:leafColumnIndex ?j .
    FILTER NOT EXISTS {
      ?prior tab:hasLeafCell ?pc2 .
      ?pc2 tab:leafColumnIndex ?j .
      ?pc2 tab:leafCellText ?t2 .
      ?cc2 tab:leafCellText ?t2 .
      ?pc2 tab:originAgreesWith ?cc2 .
    }
  }
  # CLAUSE (d) — the author's drawn grid is the same grid: set-wise agreement, both directions
  # (every boundary on each side has SOME agreeing counterpart on the other — a mark drawn twice
  # at one x is one mark, and is tolerated).
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    ?prior tab:hasAuthorBoundary ?pb1 .
    FILTER NOT EXISTS {
      ?continuation tab:hasAuthorBoundary ?cb1 .
      ?pb1 tab:boundaryAgreesWith ?cb1 .
    }
  }
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    ?continuation tab:hasAuthorBoundary ?cb2 .
    FILTER NOT EXISTS {
      ?prior tab:hasAuthorBoundary ?pb2 .
      ?pb2 tab:boundaryAgreesWith ?cb2 .
    }
  }
}
